{
  "image_name": "1beb8970761319028962e96084de08b0.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes Epstein–Barr virus (EBV) infection of B cells and the different viral programs that follow. EBV first infects a naïve B cell through the CD21 receptor, with a short lytic activation, and then persists in the nucleus as an episome. From there, the virus can adopt several latency states that differ by how many viral genes are expressed.\n\nLatency 0 appears to be the most restricted program and is labeled as predominant in health. Latency I shows limited expression, mainly including EBNA1, and is linked to Burkitt lymphoma and gastric adenocarcinoma. Latency II adds more latent proteins, including EBNA2 and membrane proteins such as LMP1 and LMP2a, and is associated with Hodgkin lymphoma and nasopharyngeal carcinoma. Latency III shows the broadest latent expression, including multiple EBNAs, and is associated with PTLD and LCL.\n\nThe diagram also shows that EBV can leave latency and enter either an abortive lytic cycle, where multiple lytic genes are expressed without forming new virions, or full lytic reactivation, with expression of more than 90 genes and production of a new generation of virus. Notes at the bottom connect EBV infection and reactivation with autoimmune diseases and EBV-positive tumors.",
